H6). The opponent has a strong presence on the left side (columns 1-3) and center. Several corners (A1, H1, H8) are still empty.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(H, 2)**: This is a safe edge move. It places a piece at H2, adjacent to my existing H3-H6 wall. It flips G3. Since H1 is empty and I control the rest of the H row, this move is very stable and does not allow the opponent to flank or take the H1 corner.\n- **(C, 8)**: This move flips D7 and connects pieces on the right edge. However, it creates a configuration that allows the opponent to play G8, which would flip the bottom right edge (D8-F8) and potentially threaten the H8 corner. This is strategically risky.\n- **(A, 6)**: Places a piece on the top edge. However, this might allow the opponent to play A7, sandwiching my new piece and flipping a sequence on the A-row.\n- **(G, 7)** and **(G, 8)**: These moves are adjacent to the empty H8 corner. Playing them would provide the opponent with an anchor to capture the H8 corner, which is a major blunder.\n- **(B, 1)**: This is a C-square move adjacent to the empty A1 corner. It allows the opponent to capture A1 immediately (A1 -> B1* -> C1@), so it is a blunder.\n\nReason: Move **(H, 2)** is the best choice because it maximizes stability. It extends my control of the bottom edge (creating a wall from H2 to H6) without exposing any weaknesses or giving the opponent access to corners. It is a quiet, consolidating move that restricts the opponent's options while securing territory.\n\nAnswer: (H, 2)"}
