nent.\n- (G, 7) is a blunder because it places a piece adjacent to the empty H8 corner while the opponent has an anchor at F6. This would allow the opponent to play H8 immediately, sandwiching G7 and taking the corner.\n- (H, 7) is a safe but passive move; it does not remove the opponent's presence on the bottom edge.\n- (G, 8) is a very strong move. It flips the opponent's pieces on the bottom edge (D8, E8, F8) as well as F7. By doing so, it connects my pieces from A8 through G8, creating a solid, stable line of pieces on the bottom row.\n\nReason: (G, 8) is the optimal choice because it consolidates the bottom edge, turning the entire row (A8-G8) into my pieces. By flipping the opponent's edge pieces to my color, I remove the anchors they would need to attack the H8 corner along that row. This move maximizes stable territory and denies the opponent tactical opportunities in that sector.\n\nAnswer: (G, 8)"}
