ponent could flip that. Need to check if a move there gives the opponent H8. G7 does lock the area.\n\n9.  **Move (H, 7):** This looks promising. It\'s a C-square and directly extends my existing edge control. A secure move. The goal is to extend that control, deny opponent corner control, so this is very strong. I can effectively lock out the opponent. If I take H7, I can deny them H8.\n\n**Analyzing the best Move**\n\nLet\'s do some more analysis, I am thinking hard, but (H, 7) is the one!\n\n**Move (H, 7)**  This one looks ideal! It flips G6 and F5, which doesn\'t change too much, and strengthens my side. I will extend the control of H-row. The opponent can\'t touch H8. This is a very secure and advantageous move. H7 makes my whole edge invincible and the H8-corner is inaccessible for the opponent.\n\n(H, 7) creates a solid barrier that is difficult for my opponent to break, forcing them to play elsewhere. This strategic move aligns with the late-game Othello principle: *maximize your moves, minimize opponents\'.* H7 secures the board and creates a path for the win. No downsides that I can see.\n\n**Other Moves:**\n\n*   (A, 6) is a potential. it has a large flip, but that could open the position.\n*   (G, 7) is adjacent to the potentially empty H8, making it an X-Square. Not ideal.\n*   F2 - is okay - but is internal. Not my priority.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nSo, the optimal move is definitely (H, 7). H7 secures my edge, makes H8 essentially mine and is low risk.\n\nFinal Answer: (H, 7)\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation:\nThe game is in the endgame phase with approximately 12 empty squares remaining. I (Player *) control the bottom edge (H1-H6) and almost the entire right column (column 8). The opponent (@) controls the center and parts of the left side. The score is close. My priority is to secure corners and stable edges while restricting the opponent\'s mobility.\n\nEvaluation:\nI have analyzed the valid moves, particularly focusing on (H, 7), (G, 7), and (A, 6).\n- **(H, 7)**: This is a C-square move on the H-row. Since I already own H1-H6 and Column 8 is fully occupied by my pieces (or effectively controlled), playing H7 creates a solid, stable wall from H1 to H7. It flips pieces G6 and F5. Crucially, it leaves the corner H8 empty but inaccessible to the opponent (they have no anchor in row H, col 8, or the diagonal). This effectively guarantees me the corner H8 later or forces the opponent to play a disadvantageous move like G7 to give me access. It is a "quiet" move that strengthens my position without exposing me to risk.\n- **(G, 7)**: This is an X-square move. While it flips several pieces (G3-G6, F6, E5) and creates a presence in the bottom right, X-squares are generally structurally weak unless they immediately take the corner. While H8 is safe for now, occupying the X-square directly is less stable than taking the edge C-square (H7) which achieves the same control.\n- **(A, 6)**: This move flips a large number of pieces (A5, A7, B7, and B6-D6). While it secures the top-right edge segment, large flips in the endgame can be dangerous as they often open up new valid moves for the opponent (mobility). It is more volatile than H7.\n\nReason:\n(H, 7) is the best move because it capitalizes on my existing control of the bottom edge and right column to lock down the bottom-right sector. It extends my stable wall on row H and renders the H8 corner safe from the opponent, effectively banking it for later. It is a risk-free, stable move that restricts the opponent\'s options.\n\nAnswer: (H, 7)'}
